Day 41 – Idempotent Consumers

In event-driven systems (especially with Apache Kafka), duplicate messages are inevitable.

🔹 ❌ Problem

A consumer may process the same event multiple times due to:

Retries

Network failures

Consumer crashes before committing offset

At-least-once delivery guarantees

👉 Result: Duplicate side effects

Double payment 💸

Duplicate orders 📦

Incorrect balances ⚠️

🔹 ✅ Solution: Idempotent Consumer

An idempotent consumer ensures that processing the same event multiple times produces the same result as processing it once.

🔹 🧠 Core Idea

👉 “Process once, ignore duplicates”

🔹 🔧 Common Implementation Patterns
1️⃣ Deduplication Table (Most Common)
📌 Approach

Store processed event IDs in DB.

Example Table:
processed_events
----------------
event_id (PK)
processed_at
Consumer Logic:
public void handleEvent(Event event) {
    if (processedEventRepo.exists(event.getId())) {
        return; // duplicate → ignore
    }

    processBusinessLogic(event);

    processedEventRepo.save(event.getId());
}
✅ Pros

Simple

Reliable

Works in fintech

❌ Cons

Extra DB writes

Table growth needs cleanup

2️⃣ Idempotency Key (Business-Level)
📌 Idea

Use a unique business key (e.g., transaction_id)

Example:
{
  "transaction_id": "TXN123",
  "amount": 100
}

DB constraint:

UNIQUE(transaction_id)

👉 Duplicate inserts automatically fail

✅ Pros

Very efficient

No extra table needed

3️⃣ Upsert / Merge (State-based systems)

Instead of “insert”, use:

INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING

OR

UPSERT

👉 Ensures same result even if repeated

4️⃣ Cache-Based Deduplication (High throughput)

Use:

Redis (TTL-based)

Flow:

Store event ID in cache

If exists → skip

⚠️ Trade-off

Faster

But not 100% reliable (cache eviction)

🔹 🔁 Where Idempotency is Critical

Payments 💳

Order processing 🛒

Account balance updates 🏦

Inventory management 📦

👉 Especially in fintech → mandatory

🔹 🔥 Real-world Flow (Kafka)

Consumer reads event

Checks event_id

If new → process + store ID

If duplicate → ignore

🔹 ⚠️ Important Notes

Kafka exactly-once ≠ business-level idempotency

Always implement idempotency at consumer level

Combine with:

Outbox Pattern

Retry + DLQ

Saga Pattern

🔹 🧩 Example (Payment System)

Event received twice:

PaymentCompleted (id=123)

Without idempotency:

Balance deducted twice ❌

With idempotency:

Second event ignored ✅

🔹 🧠 Best Practices

Use UUID for event_id

Make operations idempotent by design

Add unique constraints in DB

Keep dedup table bounded (TTL/cleanup)

Log duplicate detections

🔹 🚀 Final Summary

Duplicate events are normal

Idempotent consumer = safe processing

Techniques:

Dedup table

Idempotency key

Upsert

Cache

👉 Golden Rule:

“Never trust that an event will be delivered only once.”